 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Dem victory trap 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Democrats are poised to romp in today's election — easily winning the House and, according to most forecasts, flipping the Senate, too. Yet the party itself is less popular than the IRS. Even the GOP has a better brand, Axios' Mike Zapler, Alex Thompson and Holly Otterbein write. 

 Why it matters: There's a difference between a blowout and a mandate. Confusing the two could tempt Democrats to overread the results and forget the serious credibility problem they still face heading into 2028. 🖼️ The big picture: Democrats have done a lot of things right to position themselves for a big night. 

 They recruited candidates who've put red-state Senate races within grasp. Many of those candidates vastly outraised their Republican opponents. But polls across the board still show a lesser-of-two-evils election — or, as one Republican tied to President Trump's political operation described it, a contest of which party voters hate more. Voters are more eager to disempower Trump than to empower Democrats. By the numbers: Just 29% of registered voters have a positive opinion of the Democratic Party, compared with 38% who view the Republican Party positively, according to a pre-election NBC News poll .

 Yet the same poll shows Democrats leading the generic congressional ballot by 7 points, 52% to 45%. Mail-in ballots are processed leading up to the midterm elections at a USPS sorting facility in Golden, Colo., last week. Photo: Kevin Mohatt/Reuters 🔎 Zoom in: Many Democrats say they're determined not to repeat the mistake they made after the 2022 midterms. They defied predictions of a red wave and saw it as an endorsement of Joe Biden and their party.

 Voters were actually growing angrier, setting the stage for Trump's comeback in 2024. Democrats won the House in 2018 and impeached Trump the next year. This time, likely Speaker Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) is steering clear of impeachment talk, and his 100-day agenda is focused on affordability. Others say the party still lacks a winning pitch. "We have no economic message. And if we don't get one, we're not going to win," Democratic strategist Jim Messina told party leaders and operatives at a Third Way gathering in South Carolina earlier this year. The bottom line: Many top Democrats believe it'll take a new presidential nominee — and likely one who wins in November 2028 — to fully repair the party's damaged brand.

