 3. 🎯 Elon&#x27;s next target 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Win McNamee and Eric Thayer/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Elon Musk is looking to shake up America's wireless industry — potentially bringing the first major new competitive threat in years to a business dominated by three companies, Axios' Ina Fried writes. 

 Why it matters: The cellular industry has seen enormous technological change, but relatively little disruption to its competitive structure. Musk has the money, wireless spectrum and satellite network to attempt something different — though he faces daunting obstacles. SpaceX is assembling the pieces to become a major U.S. wireless carrier, potentially building its own terrestrial network and buying available spectrum.

 Musk may have reason to move quickly while the current administration and its regulatory appointees remain in place. 🔭 Zoom in: SpaceX has held talks about acquiring Boost Mobile or gaining access to its network arrangements, according to a Washington Post report yesterday.

 The Post reported that Verizon, AT&amp;T and T-Mobile have all rebuffed Musk's efforts to gain access to their networks. Between the lines: Musk also has a path to maneuver around them. The company could build its own network with towers and smaller rooftop antennas, though doing so would require substantial investment and time.

