 4. 🪖 Inside Pentagon&#x27;s leak probe 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Former Army Lt. Gen. Brett Sylvia pictured at Camp Swift, a small coalition base 45 miles southeast of Mosul, Iraq, in 2016. Photo: Susannah George/AP The Pentagon's former top military planner is under federal investigation and may face a court-martial for allegedly leaking to reporters from The Wall Street Journal and The New York Times, Axios' Marc Caputo reports. 

 Why it matters: Former Army Lt. Gen. Brett Sylvia was fired by Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth last week after being accused of misconduct as part of the department's war on leaks. Sylvia failed four polygraphs and admitted to the incidents during two interviews on video that totaled more than two hours, according to details from an internal Pentagon report shared with Axios.

 He allegedly shared classified information about Ukraine and Iran, which the Justice Department is investigating. Neither reporter is suspected or accused of doing anything illegal, improper or unprofessional in their reporting. Keep reading . 

 
 
